# Everything here is OPTIONAL. Boxes runs with no .env at all:
#
#   docker compose up -d        ->  http://localhost:3000
#
# Copy this file to .env only to change something. The orchestrator receives
# the whole file and validates it at boot. Unless a note says otherwise, a
# value shown commented out is the default.
#
# No credential goes here. The Claude token, the OpenAI key, the GitHub token
# and the git identity are entered on the dashboard's settings page and kept
# in the data volume, so a change needs no restart.

# ---- where the port is published ----
# compose.yaml reads these two to publish the port. Loopback by default,
# because the orchestrator has no authentication of its own. Change this only
# when something else provides that authentication. A reverse proxy on this
# host reaches 127.0.0.1. A reverse proxy in a container joins the
# boxes_default network and uses orchestrator:3000 instead.
#BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#HOST_PORT=3000

# ---- egress ----
# Hosts boxes may reach, comma or whitespace separated. Exact names and
# one-label wildcards: *.example.com matches a.example.com, but not
# a.b.example.com and not example.com. Unset is the default: any public host
# is reachable, and private ranges are still denied. The value below is an
# example list, not the default.
#
# A list never cuts a box off from a stored credential's hosts. The OpenAI
# credential also brings auth.openai.com, where Codex logs in and refreshes,
# and chatgpt.com, which a ChatGPT subscription talks to. The proxy never
# sends the credential to those two.
#
# Codex also uses files.openai.com for model attachments and ab.chatgpt.com
# for telemetry. No credential brings these two. A list that omits them makes
# the proxy refuse both, the denial counters show it, and Codex carries on.
#
# nix fetches from cache.nixos.org and channels.nixos.org, and a flake named
# github:owner/repo from github.com. Without the first two, the agent cannot
# install anything with nix.
#EGRESS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=github.com,*.github.com,*.githubusercontent.com,api.anthropic.com,api.openai.com,registry.npmjs.org,cache.nixos.org,channels.nixos.org

# ---- gitlab ----
# The GitLab the settings page token is for, as a bare hostname. Set this only
# for a self-managed instance. The proxy then swaps the token in for that host
# only, and stops intercepting gitlab.com. The instance must have a public
# address, because the proxy refuses private ranges.
#GITLAB_HOST=gitlab.com

# ---- boxes ----
# A box cannot apt-get install anything: the root filesystem is read-only, the
# agent is not root, and the box has no capabilities. The agent installs into
# /home/agent instead. To give every box a package, build an image FROM the
# published one and name it here.
#BOX_IMAGE=ghcr.io/splitbrain/boxes/box:latest
#
# The uid and gid a box runs as, and so the owner of every file in a
# workspace. 1020 keeps clear of 1000, which is the base image's own user and
# often a person on the host. The box image must be built on the same numbers,
# through its AGENT_UID and AGENT_GID build args, because a new box's home is a
# copy of the image's /home/agent and keeps the image's owner. Set these to
# the uid the orchestrator runs as to let the orchestrator drop root.
#BOX_UID=1020
#BOX_GID=1020
# How often the orchestrator pulls the box image again, so a tag like :latest
# moves here too. It also pulls at every boot, so a restart picks up a new
# image at once. A running box keeps its image until it is next started. 0
# turns the refresh off, boot included, which suits an image built on this
# host. A missing image is still pulled once, because no box can be created
# without it.
#BOX_IMAGE_PULL_MINUTES=60
# Whether the orchestrator removes the copy of the box image that a pull
# replaced. Each copy is a gigabyte or two, and nothing else reclaims it. The
# orchestrator removes only superseded copies of the box image. The daemon
# refuses to remove one a container still uses, and that copy goes after the
# box next starts. Turn this off on a host that keeps old images to roll back
# to.
#BOX_IMAGE_PRUNE=true
#BOX_SUBNET_POOL=10.200.0.0/16
#BOX_MEM_LIMIT=4g
#BOX_CPUS=2
#BOX_PIDS_LIMIT=512
#IDLE_STOP_MINUTES=30

# How often the orchestrator asks a box what work is still running in it, in
# seconds: a command left running, or a monitor watching something. Each ask
# is one Docker API call per running box. Nothing reports the end of that
# work, so this reading is also what clears a thread's list of running work.
# The orchestrator polls on this clock while a browser is attached, and asks
# at each reaper sweep otherwise.
#BACKGROUND_POLL_SECONDS=20

# AGENT_QUIET_SECONDS is how long a thread must stay silent before the agent
# counts as stopped. It is a fallback for an adapter that does not mark the
# end of a cycle itself. Codex threads always rely on it.
#
# AGENT_SETTLE_SECONDS is how long a thread must stay quiet before a push
# notification says the turn is over. It is longer, because the screen can
# correct itself and a push notification cannot.
#AGENT_QUIET_SECONDS=3
#AGENT_SETTLE_SECONDS=30

# Largest single file a prompt may carry into a workspace, in MiB. Attachments
# are written to .boxes/attachments/ in the box's own workspace.
#MAX_ATTACHMENT_MB=25

# ---- permissions ----
# hold | deny: what to do with a permission request nobody answered in time.
#PERMISSION_FALLBACK=hold
#PERMISSION_HOLD_MINUTES=120

# ---- notifications ----
# Web Push needs no configuration: the orchestrator generates a VAPID keypair
# in DATA_DIR when a browser first asks for it. This is the contact a push
# service uses when it has a problem with the deployment. It must be a mailto:
# or an https: URL.
#PUSH_SUBJECT=https://github.com/splitbrain/boxes

# ---- logging ----
# Lowest severity written to stderr: debug, info, warn or error.
#
# debug logs every ACP message forwarded in either direction, which shows
# what an agent and the adapter said to each other. It is a lot of output on
# a busy deployment.
#LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---- storage ----
# A box's workspace is a directory under DATA_DIR, bind-mounted into its
# container, so the orchestrator can read the agent's files without an exec.
# The Docker daemon resolves the bind source, so the path must be the one the
# daemon knows DATA_DIR by. The orchestrator finds it at boot by inspecting its
# own container. With the shipped compose file, that path is
# /var/lib/docker/volumes/boxes-data/_data.
#
# Set this only where that cannot work: a nested or rootless daemon, or a
# compose file that mounts a real host directory for /data.
#HOST_DATA_DIR=
